/-!
# SelRange7: the `r = 7` range terms of the selected shapes (package SelRange7)

`RebuildBound.SelRange7 : SelRange7_statement`: each of the four `r = 7` shapes of
`selBudgets` (digit 7, carry bits 7, 10, 13, 16) meets its range budget. Generated by
`RebuildBound/Proofs/SelRange7/gen.py`; do not edit.

**Route.** `selRange g = D 2^t Σ_{a<7} stopLoss(rangeLawR 64 32 P 2^I t 7 q_a) 64` with
`t = k - I`, `P = earlyCap`, `q_a = qMass 64 k 7 a`. As `2^M ≡ 1 (mod 7)` for `M = 64 - k`,
class `0` has count `⌊2^M/7⌋ + 1` and classes `1 … 6` have `⌊2^M/7⌋` (NCA5's
`qCount_eq`): two class laws per shape, and the class sum is at most `x_A + 6 x_B`
(`Glue.lean`, `selRange7_le`). Each class law is bounded as in NCA5, generalized over
`r`, `t`, `P`, `g` and the mass (`Class.lean`, `class_bound_core`, `class_bound7`): P10r
at `H = 256` (its truncation term is at most `10^-50`), NCA1's depth-`t` tree table
(bounded by NCA1's level theorems through SelRange1's `bnd4` … `bnd6`; no tree level is
rechecked), NCA5's `checkThin` and six `checkConv` (partial sums `2 … 7`, each restricted
to totals `≤ 256`), and NCA1's `checkFinal` on the root-loss marginal with
`p = q 2^-t`. The two masses of a shape differ by `2^-M`, so one thinned table (the
entrywise maximum of the two upward roundings) bounds both thinned laws: the six
partial sums are checked once per shape, the thinning and the final check once per
mass. The old-map density (`d - 1 = 6`) is bounded as in SelRange1,
`D ≤ 2^m / (2^m - n (num - 2^m))`, `D - 1 ≤ 9 · 10^-8`.

| shape   | `t` | `P`  | `g`    | class law (certified) | range term (with `D`, targets) | budget      | margin |
|---------|-----|------|--------|-----------------------|--------------------------------|-------------|--------|
| L2 d7   | 4   | 256  | `2^3`  | `4.2821924351e-34`    | `4.796064e-32`                 | `4.93e-32`  | 2.72 % |
| L3 d7   | 4   | 448  | `2^6`  | `3.8400336278e-31`    | `4.300845e-29`                 | `4.37e-29`  | 1.58 % |
| L4 d7   | 5   | 1088 | `2^8`  | `1.8425005296e-29`    | `4.127222e-27`                 | `4.18e-27`  | 1.26 % |
| L5 d7   | 6   | 1408 | `2^10` | `5.7426932925e-30`    | `2.572730e-27`                 | `2.61e-27`  | 1.43 % |

(The two class laws of a shape agree to 12 digits.) The certified range terms lie below
the Python certifier's (`4.8770e-32`, `4.3171e-29`, `4.1337e-27`, `2.5752e-27`), which
majorizes `(1 + W)^g` by `exp(g W)` and uses `λ = 32 + 2^-36`.

**Kernel cost** (`decide +kernel`; "type checking" time from the profiler, the four
shape modules run as four parallel `lean` processes on this host with
`LEAN_NAT_MAX_SIZE = 10^9`; peak RSS from `/usr/bin/time`). Each shape module
elaborates its ten checks one at a time (`Elab.async false`), so its peak memory is
that of the thinning (about 17 GB) rather than the sum of its checks.

| module | thin A | thin B | conv 2 … 7                          | final A, B | kernel | wall  | peak RSS |
|--------|--------|--------|-------------------------------------|------------|--------|-------|----------|
| `L2`   | 18.5 s | 13.7 s | 10.0, 14.8, 17.3, 18.7, 19.1, 20.0 s | 2.5, 2.3 s | 137 s  | 142 s | 21.2 GB  |
| `L3`   | 17.3 s | 13.3 s | 10.0, 14.7, 17.2, 18.6, 19.0, 20.3 s | 2.7, 2.6 s | 136 s  | 141 s | 20.7 GB  |
| `L4`   | 16.7 s | 13.0 s | 9.9, 14.7, 17.2, 18.5, 18.7, 19.4 s  | 2.9, 2.9 s | 134 s  | 139 s | 19.0 GB  |
| `L5`   | 16.3 s | 12.4 s | 10.0, 14.8, 17.2, 18.6, 18.7, 19.3 s | 3.0, 3.0 s | 133 s  | 138 s | 18.9 GB  |

Total kernel time 540 s; `Class`, `Glue` and this module add 0.3 s. The 28 tables
(`Tab<S>Th`, `Tab<S>S<n>`, 0.2–0.5 MB of source each) elaborate in about 8–13 s and
1 GB each. With lake the whole package builds in about 3 minutes wall (peak 21 GB per
process); `lake env leanchecker` replays the four shape modules in parallel.

**Probability obstacles:** none beyond NCA1's and NCA5's; Mathlib's
measures throughout (`binomial`, `Measure.conv`, `Measure.bind`, `lintegral`), no PMF
fallback.
-/

